WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.620
Film Courage: What are three mistakes screenwriters
make with loglines?

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:05.960
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting teacher
and Consultant: The three that I tend to see

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:09.360
are stopping at the setup.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:15.679
So describing just the initial situation and
not really giving us any indication of what

00:00:15.679 --> 00:00:22.529
is the meat of the movie if that makes sense?

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:29.350
Often when we come at stories, we come with
an interesting what if and that is a great

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:34.410
entry point into a story but then you sort
of have to develop that continuation of the

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:35.410
conflict, right?

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:37.590
What is the real problem that they are trying
to solve?

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:41.500
In a logline if you just give me the setup
I don’t know if you’ve thought that through.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.241
If you’ve come up with the story part of
your screenplay I know that you’ve come

00:00:46.241 --> 00:00:51.740
up with a situation but not necessarily a
story so that is the first mistake that I

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:54.809
see people make with loglines.

00:00:54.809 --> 00:01:02.280
The second one is not having an act two which
is a little bit different from the first one.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.850
If you’re conveying to me what’s happening
in your movie but it’s something sort of

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:12.240
transitory or that it can be done in one scene
or one minute then that raises a little bit

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.389
of a red flag because then it feels like maybe
you don’t have enough to sustain an entire

00:01:17.389 --> 00:01:18.569
movie.

00:01:18.569 --> 00:01:25.649
The third mistake I see a lot is packing too
much detail into your longline.

00:01:25.649 --> 00:01:29.560
Usually this happens after somebody has written
the script and so they know so much about

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.419
the story and they’ve fallen in love with
everything that they’ve written then they

00:01:34.419 --> 00:01:37.990
try to distill all of that down into one sentence
and it’s really hard because they have all

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:42.079
these details that they know and all of these
subplots and supporting characters and character

00:01:42.079 --> 00:01:44.170
arcs and all this stuff.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:48.609
So they will try to jam all of this into one
sentence and that just makes it confusing

00:01:48.609 --> 00:01:55.359
and we want that logline to ultimately just
really convey a solid sense of what that movie

00:01:55.359 --> 00:01:57.919
we will be watching if we sit down in the
theater.

00:01:57.919 --> 00:02:02.359
So we need to know what is that main conflict
(that big conflict) that we’re going to

00:02:02.359 --> 00:02:06.840
be tracking over the course of that movie
and have a sense of the nature of the action

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.580
that we’re watching on screen.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.610
What are sort of the broad strokes things
that that character is doing in pursuit of

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:14.930
that story goal.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:19.780
That’s really what you’re trying to get
into the logline and like I will say you can

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:25.040
start with those sort of 4 foundational elements,
your protagonist, the story goal, the antagonist

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:27.930
or main force of opposition and the stakes.

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:33.870
Start there and write one sentence with those
elements and that should give you a really

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.099
good start for your logline.

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:39.629
You may need to add a few details but that
way you’re starting with a solid foundation

00:02:39.629 --> 00:02:44.500
and then you can add details as necessary
rather than starting by putting everything

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.409
you know about the story into a story and
then trying to pull things out.

00:02:47.409 --> 00:02:52.280
Film Courage: Going back to the logline example
that we did (and I don’t know if this video

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.890
will come out before the other one referring
to this).

00:02:54.890 --> 00:03:00.569
People might be confused to what I’m referring
to but the logline that I gave you was definitely

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:02.999
confusing, there was too much (information)
in it.

00:03:02.999 --> 00:03:06.030
So we cleaned it up and we found out that
it wasn’t just about the writing group,

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.109
it was more about her finding love.

00:03:08.109 --> 00:03:09.670
So that was getting to the meat of it?

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:15.719
Naomi: Yes, because I think if you were going
to write that script it sounded like the story

00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:20.469
that you wanted to tell was really about this
mismatched romance and so that’s what we

00:03:20.469 --> 00:03:23.870
want to convey in the logline because then
when I read the script or I watch the movie

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.680
that’s the movie that I’m getting.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.469
You’re not telling me that DANGEROUS MINDS
story.

00:03:31.469 --> 00:03:38.450
You’re telling me THE ENGLISH PATIENT or
whatever and those are both movies that work

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:42.121
but I want to know which one I’m getting
before I pick up that script or before I go

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:47.749
see that movie so making sure that you convey
a true sense of the story you want to tell

00:03:47.749 --> 00:03:50.420
in that logline, that’s what we’re looking
for.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:54.469
Film Courage: What situation will a screenwriter
use loglines?

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:55.469
When will this come up?

00:03:55.469 --> 00:03:59.720
Naomi: I think there are a few different situations.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:04.019
In the classes that I teach we usually we
usually start with logline as the first iteration

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:05.019
of your story.

00:04:05.019 --> 00:04:09.840
So just like you and I were talking about
what’s that story that you actually want

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.909
to write, we use loglines as a way to figure
that out.

00:04:13.909 --> 00:04:16.829
What is this story that you’re trying to
tell.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:25.090
In that case we put more of the focus on getting
those essentials of the story into the logline

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:30.020
so we can tell if that foundation is solid
before we start developing the story further.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:36.960
But you might also use a logline in a query
letter or to pitch to somebody in person (that

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.300
elevator pitch of the story).

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:43.419
So if you’re using that version of the logline
you’re probably going to think a little

00:04:43.419 --> 00:04:47.330
more carefully about the language that you
use because it’s not for internal use anymore

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:51.449
you are actually presenting this as this is
the product essentially that I’m trying

00:04:51.449 --> 00:04:52.449
to pitch you.

00:04:52.449 --> 00:04:56.560
So you will probably be a little bit more
careful about word choice and a little more

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:02.430
conscious of getting or really conveying the
tone of the story and that feel of the movie

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:07.580
if you can through the word choice and through
the language and the way you’re describing

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:08.580
the situation.

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:10.420
Film Courage: Can you explain foundation?

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:13.430
I realize this is jumping a little bit off
topic.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:18.729
Naomi: Yes, when I talk about the foundation
that is just me referring to the kind of essential

00:05:18.729 --> 00:05:19.970
building blocks of your story.

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:26.270
So the protagonist, the story goal, the antagonist
or the opposition and the stakes.

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:29.190
Those are the things that every story needs.

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:34.220
You kind of have to have those elements and
so that is where I start if I’m helping

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:39.639
someone figure out what story they are telling
or what’s not working about their story,

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:43.430
you just have to start with the foundation
layer and then work your way up right?

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.440
So we make sure that that is solid and then
we talk about the other pieces that go into

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:48.440
your story work.

00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:52.650
Film Courage: What if someone says “I don’t
have a really strong antagonist but I have

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:54.480
a really great sidekick character.”

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:55.800
Is this because there is no opposition?

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.389
There is nothing that this hero needs to overcome?

00:05:58.389 --> 00:05:59.780
Naomi: Yes, that could be.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.659
That would be my first instinct.

00:06:01.659 --> 00:06:10.960
If someone says they are writing that doesn’t
have an antagonist it wouldn’t necessarily

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:16.000
be a deal breaker but I would want to know
where is that conflict coming from right?

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:22.551
Because if you are telling a story where sort
of nothing goes wrong it’s probably either

00:06:22.551 --> 00:06:27.419
going to be a pretty short story or we are
just going to get bored pretty quick.

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:30.710
So I would question where is that conflict
coming from?

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:37.960
If it’s not sort of a traditional direct
conflict that we can see between two people

00:06:37.960 --> 00:06:44.560
or a person or a group or that type of thing,
then what is making this journey for your

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:47.870
protagonist so difficult or so interesting?

